UNIT isExtglob
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG isExtglob(str: any) -> boolean
DEPENDS-ON none

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PURPOSE
#   Returns true iff `str` is a JS string that contains an UNESCAPED extglob
#   opening: one of the chars @ ? ! + * immediately followed by '(' AND with a
#   matching ')' somewhere later on the same line. Backslash-escaped characters
#   are skipped (an escape consumes the next char), so escape-parity decides
#   whether a following extglob opener counts. Any non-string, and the empty
#   string, return false.
#
# THE EXACT REGEX (verbatim, load-bearing — reproduce byte-for-byte):
#   /(\\).|([@?!+*]\(.*\))/g
#   Decomposed:
#     ALT-1  (\\).      group 1 = a literal backslash; then ANY ONE char.
#                       NOTE: '.' (no /s flag) matches any char EXCEPT newline,
#                       and the '.' is REQUIRED — a lone trailing backslash with
#                       no following char does NOT match ALT-1.
#                       group 1 captures only the backslash; the following char
#                       is consumed by the bare '.' but not captured.
#     ALT-2  ([@?!+*]\(.*\))
#                       group 2 = one of the chars in the class [@ ? ! + *],
#                       then a literal '(', then '.*' (GREEDY, any chars except
#                       newline, zero or more), then a literal ')'.
#                       Because '.*' is greedy it spans to the LAST ')' on that
#                       newline-free segment.
#   The 'g' flag is present but IRRELEVANT to behavior: a FRESH RegExp object is
#   constructed each loop iteration (regex literal evaluated inside the while
#   condition), so lastIndex always starts at 0; forward progress is made solely
#   by str = str.slice(...). A re-emitter MAY use a non-global regex with .exec
#   or .match — observable behavior is identical as long as it finds the
#   LEFTMOST match and the two capture groups, and re-runs from a sliced suffix.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BEHAVIOR
  # ---- NODE 1: type / empty guard ----
  # if (typeof str !== 'string' || str === '') return false
  # This is the FIRST check. Anything that is not a primitive string value
  # (number, null, undefined, plain object, array, boolean, NaN, RegExp,
  #  Symbol, function, boxed String object, ...) -> false.
  # The empty string '' -> false.
  IF (type-of str) != "string"  -> return false
  IF str == ""                  -> return false

  # ---- NODE 2: scan loop (leftmost-match, escape-skip, slice-advance) ----
  # Repeat until no match:
  #   match := LEFTMOST match of /(\\).|([@?!+*]\(.*\))/ in str
  #   (i.e. RegExp.prototype.exec on a fresh global regex, or equivalent .match)
  #
  #   IF no match            -> return false   (loop exits)
  #
  #   IF match captured group 2 (the extglob alternative ALT-2)
  #                          -> return true    (immediately)
  #
  #   ELSE  (match captured group 1 — an escape "\X" via ALT-1)
  #        # advance past the entire consumed match, then continue scanning.
  #        str := str.slice(match.index + match[0].length)
  #        # match[0] for ALT-1 is 2 chars: the backslash + the next char.
  #        # match.index is where that match started.
  #        loop
  #
  # KEY POINT ON ALTERNATION PRIORITY:
  #   The regex picks the LEFTMOST match position. At a given leftmost position,
  #   the regex tries ALT-1 first then ALT-2 (left-to-right alternation), but
  #   because the engine finds the overall-leftmost match, what matters is which
  #   alternative can match at the earliest index. A backslash earlier in the
  #   string yields an ALT-1 match before any later extglob; the loop then slices
  #   past it (consuming the escaped char) and re-scans the remainder.
  #
  # WHY ESCAPE-PARITY DECIDES (worked examples — all VERIFIED against package):
  #   '\@(a)'      : leftmost match is ALT-1 "\@" at idx0 (greedily the engine
  #                  cannot match ALT-2 at idx0 because idx0 is '\'; ALT-1 matches
  #                  "\@"); slice -> "(a)"; rescan "(a)" -> no opener char before
  #                  '(' -> no match -> FALSE.
  #   '\\@(a)'     : ALT-1 matches "\\" (backslash+backslash) at idx0; slice ->
  #                  "@(a)"; rescan -> ALT-2 "@(a)" -> TRUE.
  #   '\\\@(a)'    : (3 backslashes) ALT-1 "\\" -> slice "\@(a)" -> ALT-1 "\@" ->
  #                  slice "(a)" -> FALSE.
  #   '\\\\@(a)'   : (4 backslashes) two "\\" pairs consumed -> "@(a)" -> TRUE.
  #   'z\@(a)'     : leftmost ALT-1 is "\@" at idx1; slice -> "(a)" -> FALSE.
  #   'z\@(a)@(b)' : ALT-1 "\@" -> slice "(a)@(b)" -> rescan -> ALT-2 "@(b)" -> TRUE.
  #   '\a'         : ALT-1 "\a" consumes whole string -> slice "" -> no match -> FALSE.
  #   '\'   (lone) : ALT-1 needs a char after '\'; none -> no match -> FALSE.
  #   'abc\'       : trailing lone backslash, ALT-1 can't complete -> no match -> FALSE.
  #
  # NEWLINE BEHAVIOR ('.' excludes newline, no /s flag) — VERIFIED:
  #   '@(a\nb)'    : after "@(", '.*' can only match "a" (stops at newline);
  #                  the ')' after the newline is unreachable in one '.*' span ->
  #                  ALT-2 cannot complete on that segment -> no match -> FALSE.
  #   'a\n@(b)'    : leftmost ALT-2 "@(b)" sits on the 2nd line entirely -> TRUE.
  #   '@(ab)\nc'   : "@(ab)" completes before the newline -> TRUE.
  #
  # GREEDY '.*' (spans to LAST ')' on the segment) — VERIFIED:
  #   '@(a) and *(b)' -> ALT-2 group2 spans "@(a) and *(b)" greedily -> TRUE.
  #   '@(a)b)c'       -> ALT-2 group2 = "@(a)b)c" (greedy to last ')') -> TRUE.
  #   '@(a)@(b)'      -> TRUE (group2 matches first opener through last ')').
  #
  # OPENER-WITHOUT-PAREN / PAREN-WITHOUT-OPENER (no ALT-2) — VERIFIED:
  #   '@(a'   -> '(' present but no ')'  -> ALT-2 fails -> FALSE.
  #   '@('    -> no ')'                  -> FALSE.
  #   '@x(y)' -> '@' not immediately followed by '(' -> FALSE.
  #   '*('    -> no ')'                  -> FALSE.
  #   '@?!+*' -> no '(' after any opener -> FALSE.
  #   '@(?(!(+(*(' -> after first "@(", '.*' is greedy but there is NO ')' at all
  #                  -> ALT-2 fails -> FALSE.
  #   '*.js'  -> '*' not followed by '(' -> FALSE.
  #
  # EMPTY-BODY EXTGLOBS ('.*' allows zero chars) — VERIFIED:
  #   '@()'  -> ALT-2 "@()" with empty body -> TRUE.
  #   '*()'  -> TRUE.
  #
  # ALL FIVE OPENER CHARS — VERIFIED each:
  #   '@(pattern)' TRUE, '?(pattern)' TRUE, '!(pattern)' TRUE,
  #   '+(pattern)' TRUE, '*(pattern)' TRUE.
  #
  # TERMINATION: the loop always makes forward progress — ALT-2 returns true
  # immediately; ALT-1 consumes >=2 chars (backslash + one char) so str strictly
  # shrinks; no-match returns false. No infinite loop is possible.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
  # Pure deterministic function of its single argument. No seam (no clock/RNG/
  # io/net). The oracle records expected boolean (or {__throw} — but NOTE this
  # function NEVER throws for any codec-faithful input; the type guard returns
  # false for all non-strings). Compare expected === actual.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOPE
  IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
    - The two-clause NODE 1 guard: (typeof str !== 'string') OR (str === '')
      -> false, evaluated BEFORE any scanning.
    - The exact regex /(\\).|([@?!+*]\(.*\))/  with its two capture groups,
      its '.'-excludes-newline semantics, and greedy '.*'.
    - The leftmost-match + group2-wins-immediately + slice-advance-on-group1
      scan loop, with escape-parity as the emergent consequence.
    - Opener char class is EXACTLY [@ ? ! + *] (at-sign, question, bang, plus,
      star). No other char (e.g. '(' alone, '$', '^') is an opener.
  CARRIED DATA: none. (No literal tables.)
  OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs because
  they do not survive the JSON oracle codec, but a blind re-emitter must still
  handle them per NODE 1):
    - Symbol argument: typeof is "symbol" -> NODE 1 -> false. (Symbols are not
      JSON-codec-faithful; documented here, not generated.)
    - function argument: typeof "function" -> false. (Not codec-faithful.)
    - boxed String object (new String("@(a)")): typeof is "object" -> false
      (NOT "string") -> false. (Boxed primitives are not codec-faithful.)
    - undefined / NaN / +-Infinity as the argument: typeof "undefined"/"number"
      -> false. These are non-round-trippable as standalone JSON args, so they
      are described here but NOT emitted; behavior is still false.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RE-EMITTER DIRECTIVE (default export):
#   The emission is an ES module loaded via import(). This unit is the package's
#   DEFAULT export (module.exports = function isExtglob(str){...}).
#   The blind re-emitter MUST expose the function as the module DEFAULT export:
#       export default function isExtglob(str) { ... }
#   (or `const isExtglob = ...; export default isExtglob;`). It must NOT be a
#   named export. Signature: one positional argument, returns a primitive boolean.
#   PURE: no state, no mutation of the argument, no deep-clone needed.

